1.What are the different software models available?

Software development models are structured approaches used to plan, design, develop, test, and deploy software applications.
These models define how different phases of SDLC are organized and executed.

1. Waterfall Model
The Waterfall model is a sequential model where each phase is completed before moving to the next phase. 
It follows a fixed flow such as requirement analysis, design, development, testing, deployment, and maintenance.
It is easy to manage but not flexible for changing requirements.

2. V-Model (Verification and Validation Model)
The V-Model is an extension of the Waterfall model where testing activities are planned in parallel with development phases.
Each development phase has a corresponding testing phase, which helps in early defect detection and better quality assurance.

3. Agile Model
The Agile model follows an iterative and incremental approach where the application is developed in small modules called sprints. 
It supports frequent changes, continuous feedback, and faster delivery. Agile is widely used in modern software projects.

4. Spiral Model
The Spiral model combines iterative development and risk analysis. It focuses on identifying and reducing risks in each cycle.
This model is suitable for large and high-risk projects.

5. Incremental Model
In the Incremental model, the software is developed and delivered in small functional parts. Each increment adds new features and improves the existing system.
This allows early delivery of working software.

6. Prototype Model
The Prototype model involves creating a working prototype of the application to understand customer requirements clearly.
User feedback is collected and the prototype is refined before actual development begins.

7. RAD Model (Rapid Application Development)
The RAD model focuses on fast development and quick delivery using reusable components and rapid prototyping.
It is suitable for projects with tight deadlines.

8. DevOps Model
The DevOps model integrates development and operations teams to enable continuous integration, continuous testing, and continuous deployment.
It improves collaboration and speeds up software delivery.

2.What is Agile? Explain about different frameworks in Agile

Agile is a software development methodology that follows an iterative and incremental approach where the application is developed in small cycles called sprints.
Agile focuses on continuous customer feedback, fast delivery, collaboration between teams, and flexibility to handle changing requirements.

1. Scrum Framework

Scrum is the most widely used Agile framework. It divides work into time-boxed iterations called sprints, usually lasting 2 to 4 weeks.
Scrum uses defined roles such as Product Owner, Scrum Master, and Development Team. It includes ceremonies like Sprint Planning, Daily Stand-up, Sprint Review,
and Sprint Retrospective. Scrum focuses on fast delivery, transparency, and continuous improvement.

2. Kanban Framework

Kanban is a visual workflow management system that uses a Kanban board to track tasks in columns such as To Do, In Progress, and Done.
Unlike Scrum, Kanban does not use fixed sprints and supports continuous delivery. It focuses on limiting work in progress and improving workflow efficiency.
